<details>
<summary><b>Steps of Reproduction ✅ </b></summary>

```mdx
1. Open a dashboard and click "Edit dashboard properties" to open `PropertiesModal`
(`superset-frontend/src/dashboard/components/PropertiesModal/index.tsx`), which fetches
data from `/api/v1/dashboard/<id>` and calls `handleDashboardData` around lines 88–135,
setting `owners` state via `setOwners(owners)` with owner objects that include `email`.

2. In the modal's "Access & ownership" section rendered by `AccessSection` (passed
`owners` and `onChangeOwners` at the bottom of `index.tsx`), modify the owners list (e.g.,
add or remove an owner) so the owners AsyncSelect triggers the `onChangeOwners` callback.

3. The callback `handleOnChangeOwners` at lines ~232–255 receives `(owners, options)` and
maps each selection to `{ id, full_name }`, explicitly omitting the `email` field defined
in the `Owners` type, then calls `setOwners(parsedOwners)`, replacing previously
email-rich owner objects with ones containing only `id` and `full_name`.

4. After this state update, React re-renders `AccessSection` with the updated `owners`
prop that now lacks `email`, so any UI in the owners selector (using `OwnerSelectLabel`
from `src/features/owners/OwnerSelectLabel`) that relies on `owner.email` or associated
option metadata to render the secondary username line no longer has that data, and the
username/secondary text disappears for the modified owners until the modal is closed and
reopened (which reloads full owner data via `handleDashboardData`).
```
</details>
