### Inefficient array processing in compound filters <sub>![category Performance](https://img.shields.io/badge/Performance-4f46e5)</sub>

<details>
  <summary>Tell me more</summary>

###### What is the issue?
The compound filter processing uses a map-then-filter pattern that creates an intermediate array of all clauses before filtering out empty ones.


###### Why this matters
This approach allocates unnecessary memory for empty clause strings and performs two array iterations instead of one, reducing performance when processing compound filters with many conditions.

###### Suggested change ∙ *Feature Preview*
Use reduce to build the result array in a single pass:

```typescript
const clauses = conditions.reduce((acc, cond) => {
  const clause = simpleFilterToWhereClause(columnName, cond);
  if (clause !== '') {
    acc.push(clause);
  }
  return acc;
}, [] as string[]);
```


